I wrote the report on "Matrix Theory Reloaded: A BPS Road to Holography" and saved it as `report.tex`. It compiles with pdflatex to a 2-page `report.pdf`. The paper was too large to read in one pass (336 KB), so I read the abstract, introduction, section list, conjecture setup, flow-equation section and Outlook. The detailed derivations in between I only skimmed.

- **Principal achievement:** The paper ties matrix theory, AdS/CFT, non-Lorentzian geometry and $T\bar T$ deformations together under one idea, BPS decoupling limits. Its key observation is that the limit taken far from the branes is the same as the bulk near-horizon limit. That gives a systematic, top-down way to produce new holographic dualities, plus new $T\bar T$-like flow equations for higher-dimensional branes.
- **Principal drawback:** The headline conjecture (holography as a DLCQ$^n$/DLCQ$^m$ correspondence) and the new non-Lorentzian bulk geometries rest mostly on kinematics and analogy:
  - the new geometries involve rescaling $N$ by hand;
  - nobody has checked they solve the supergravity equations;
  - no observables are matched;
  - the analysis covers only the Poincaré patch;
  - the flow equations are derived only for abelian, bosonic actions.
- **Recommended follow-up:** Study the simple three-dimensional flow equation that the paper finds for both the D2- and M2-brane, treated as a field-theory deformation in its own right. Test whether it is solvable the way 2d $T\bar T$ is, then extend it to supersymmetric and non-abelian theories (3d SYM, BLG/ABJM). This is feasible because it doesn't depend on the unfinished supergravity framework, and it has a clear payoff either way. The report also names a more ambitious second option: building that supergravity framework to check the new geometries directly.

The build also left `report.aux` and `report.log` in the working directory.

